WEBVTT

00:01.500 --> 00:04.645
Hi! One of my hobbies is running the lights in a theatre,

00:04.695 --> 00:07.497
and over the years I've tried quite a few setups.

00:07.547 --> 00:12.175
I started with hardware lighting desks, and they were never very practical for me,

00:12.225 --> 00:15.141
because I'm never standing still in the same place.

00:15.191 --> 00:18.621
Lately I've been using an iPad with an app called StageLight.

00:18.671 --> 00:22.272
It's a really good app, and if you have an iPad, I recommend it.

00:22.322 --> 00:28.833
But I can't always have the iPad with me, and a lot of the time I just need to check something quickly on the spot,

00:28.883 --> 00:33.340
or set up a scene on stage — and my iPhone would be the perfect tool for that.

00:33.390 --> 00:36.420
But there was nothing for the iPhone that did the job.

00:36.470 --> 00:39.045
So that's where this app came from: NodeLight.

00:39.095 --> 00:40.578
So let's see how it works.

00:43.103 --> 00:46.848
In this video I build a light show from scratch with NodeLight, in a real room.

00:50.078 --> 00:51.463
I start with an empty app.

00:51.513 --> 00:54.277
I tap plus and add the first light, the room light.

00:54.327 --> 00:57.202
I call it Room Top, and since it only has brightness,

00:57.252 --> 00:59.018
I keep just the dimmer channel.

01:02.018 --> 01:03.668
Every light has its own fader.

01:03.718 --> 01:07.236
I push it up… and look at the room: it lights up straight away.

01:07.286 --> 01:11.761
If I open the light, I see its DMX address and the exact value of each channel.

01:15.018 --> 01:17.165
To keep the lights tidy, I use sections.

01:17.215 --> 01:20.564
I make the first one, called Room, and move Room Top into it.

01:25.018 --> 01:28.147
The second section is for the front spotlights: Front Spots.

01:31.543 --> 01:33.747
I add the first spot inside this section.

01:34.167 --> 01:35.701
It only has a dimmer too.

01:39.518 --> 01:42.744
With so many lights on the truss, which one is my spot?

01:42.794 --> 01:44.411
I tap Locate and it flashes.

01:44.461 --> 01:48.817
If the wrong one flashes, the address isn't right: I fix it and try again.

01:48.867 --> 01:50.713
Once I've found it, I tap Stop.

02:14.518 --> 02:17.960
The other spots are the same, so I don't need to do it all again.

02:18.010 --> 02:21.667
I duplicate the spot, and the copy goes straight to the next address.

02:21.717 --> 02:23.652
Look: the spot next to it lights up.

02:29.543 --> 02:34.069
I duplicate and rename until I have all five front spots, each with its own address.

02:44.518 --> 02:47.750
With Arrange Lights I put the lights in the same order as on the truss,

02:47.800 --> 02:48.800
left to right.

02:51.853 --> 02:54.097
Sections I'm not using, I fold away.

02:54.147 --> 02:57.206
The list stays short and I find everything fast.

03:00.231 --> 03:02.818
In the Groups tab I make a group with the five spots.

03:03.238 --> 03:06.150
Now one fader controls them all, and they light up together.

03:12.566 --> 03:13.802
Now the color lights.

03:14.222 --> 03:16.147
I make a section called RGB.

03:19.172 --> 03:21.364
I add Par 1, at DMX address 301.

03:21.414 --> 03:26.037
Since it's an RGB light, the color channels are already filled in.

03:29.063 --> 03:31.904
Locate… and there it is, flashing at the end of the truss.

03:42.037 --> 03:43.932
I pick red and bring up the dimmer.

03:43.982 --> 03:47.260
The light comes on in red, right as I move it on the phone.

03:52.537 --> 03:54.792
The round button opens the color wheel.

03:54.842 --> 03:58.574
I tap a color and the light changes instantly: blue, red, green.

03:58.624 --> 04:01.755
Underneath there are ready-made colors, to go faster.

04:09.063 --> 04:11.462
Just like with the spots, I duplicate the Par.

04:11.882 --> 04:13.869
The copy even keeps the same color.

04:20.562 --> 04:23.781
And I end up with five Pars, each with its own name and address.

04:36.062 --> 04:37.866
I make a group with the five Pars.

04:42.732 --> 04:45.835
Now one color on the group changes all five lights at once.

04:46.255 --> 04:48.834
And the group's fader sets the brightness of all of them.

04:54.707 --> 04:56.657
But every light is still independent.

04:56.707 --> 04:58.548
I can paint one red, another green,

04:58.598 --> 05:01.948
another blue… and build whatever look I want, light by light.

05:13.707 --> 05:17.310
And the group's fader only changes the brightness: I bring it up and down,

05:17.360 --> 05:19.186
and every light keeps its own color.

05:22.186 --> 05:24.299
When I like a look, I save it as a preset.

05:24.349 --> 05:28.623
I give it a name, pick the transition time — two seconds here — and the fade style.

05:35.516 --> 05:39.526
The Grand Master controls the brightness of everything at once: down,

05:39.576 --> 05:41.821
and everything comes back up together.

05:41.871 --> 05:45.057
And BO is blackout: everything goes dark in an instant.

05:45.107 --> 05:47.167
Another tap, and it all comes back.

05:50.192 --> 05:52.576
I change the colors and save a second preset.

05:58.917 --> 06:03.152
Now I just tap a preset: the lights glide smoothly from one look to the other.

06:03.202 --> 06:05.289
Tap the other one, and they come back.

06:09.417 --> 06:13.126
In Effects I pick a pattern: blink, pulse, chase, sparkle or rainbow.

06:13.176 --> 06:15.360
I choose the lights or groups it runs on.

06:15.410 --> 06:19.988
And in Multiple I can mix several patterns in one effect — here, pulse with rainbow.

06:50.942 --> 06:53.302
I tap play and the effect runs on the lights.

06:53.722 --> 06:55.771
The fader next to it sets the speed.

07:20.917 --> 07:23.051
While the effect runs on the color lights,

07:23.101 --> 07:28.306
I go to Lights and move the Room fader: the room light goes up and down, and the effect keeps going.

07:28.356 --> 07:31.947
An effect only takes the lights it runs on — the rest are still yours.

07:31.997 --> 07:34.442
Stop, and everything goes back to where it was.

07:49.917 --> 07:51.098
When the show is ready,

07:51.148 --> 07:54.739
I can send it to another phone running NodeLight on the same Wi-Fi.

07:54.789 --> 07:57.626
Everything goes: lights, groups, presets and effects.

08:00.862 --> 08:03.741
Here it is with two real phones, an iPhone and an Android.

08:04.161 --> 08:06.651
I move a fader on one, and the other follows.

08:07.070 --> 08:09.455
Both control the same lights, at the same time.

08:17.837 --> 08:20.175
The hardware I used: besides the DMX lights,

08:20.225 --> 08:24.030
just an Art-Net node — this little box, the Pknight Art-Net WiFi mini.

08:24.080 --> 08:29.080
It takes the signal from the phone over Wi-Fi and passes it to the lights over the DMX cable,

08:29.130 --> 08:30.980
and NodeLight finds it on its own.

08:31.030 --> 08:34.075
If you want the same one, the link is in the description.

08:34.125 --> 08:37.658
It's an affiliate link, so buying through it supports the channel,

08:37.708 --> 08:39.065
at no extra cost to you.

08:43.872 --> 08:46.070
NodeLight is free to try with one light.

08:46.490 --> 08:49.436
A single purchase unlocks everything — no subscription.

08:52.832 --> 08:55.556
It's on the App Store, and coming soon to Google Play.

08:55.976 --> 08:58.189
Links and the manual are in the description.

08:58.609 --> 09:02.233
And if you like my work, you can always buy me a coffee; the link is there too.

09:02.653 --> 09:04.310
If you liked it, subscribe.

09:04.730 --> 09:05.730
See you soon!
